Natural enzyme-based catalytic cascades hold great promise for cancer therapy, but their clinical utility is greatly hindered by the loss of their functions during in vivo delivery. Herein, a plasmonic trienzyme-integrated metal–organic framework (plasEnMOF) nanoplatform with high-efficiency enzyme cascades is reported for synergistic starvation, chemodynamic, and plasmonic hyperthermia trimodal therapy of hypoxic tumors. These plasEnMOFs are created with encapsulation of near-infrared-II (NIR-II) plasmonic Au nanorods and natural enzymes—catalase (CAT), glucose oxidase (GOx), and horseradish peroxidase (HRP) within zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) MOFs. As a trienzyme cascade system, the plasEnMOFs effectively deplete intratumoral glucose and generate toxic reactive oxygen species (ROS) for starvation therapy and chemodynamic therapy (CDT) combined with the plasmonic hyperthermia therapy (PHT). The enhanced glucose consumption and ROS generation by the NIR-II plasmonic photothermal effect are also demonstrated. The improved chemo- and thermotolerance of the encapsulated natural enzymes within the protective ZIF-8 MOFs are evidenced. With the integrated enzyme cascades and NIR-II photothermal effects, these plasEnMOFs are demonstrated with exceptional therapeutic effects on 4T1 xenograft tumors through the combined starvation/CDT/PHT therapy. This work highlights the superiority of natural enzyme cascade systems integrated in plasmonic MOFs for high-efficiency enzymatic cancer therapy.
## 1. Introduction
The utility of natural enzymes for enzymatic oncotherapy is a promising treatment strategy owing to their high catalytic activity, superior substrate specificity, and negligible side effects.[1,2] The therapeutic efficiency of enzymatic oncotherapy greatly relies on natural enzymes, usually essential to normal functions in all living organisms.[3,4] Direct delivery of functional natural enzymes into cancer cells would trigger substantive cancer cell death, significantly promoting the therapeutic effects. In particular, multienzyme catalytic cascades represent an emerging class of enzyme therapy with enzyme-triggered sequential chemical transformations in which the product of the upstream enzyme catalytic reaction becomes the substrate of the downstream reaction until the final therapeutic agent is reached.[5–7] In multi­enzyme catalytic cascades, a series of enzymatic reactions proceed in a concurrent fashion without the separation of intermediates; the confinement of these cooperating enzymes within the compartments could yield high local concentrations of enzymes and catalytic substrates, consequently boosting the performance of the total cascade reactions.[8,9] In addition, enzyme cascades offer additional advantages for cancer therapy in terms of efficient mass transfer and minimal intermediate decomposition usually occurring at high local concentrations.[10,11] In spite of these great potentials, strict physiological conditions, expensive production cost, selective operational stability, poor reusability, and diminished catalytic activity in nonphysiological environments are the main intrinsic fragility of natural enzymes for enzymatic oncotherapy.[12,13] Considerable efforts have been devoted to the development of specialized nanomaterials as nanozymes with intrinsic enzyme-mimicking properties.[2] Nevertheless, these nanozymes have much lower enzymatic activity and substrate specificity compared with natural enzymes.[14] To this end, the natural enzyme-based strategy is being still pursued for highefficiency enzymatic oncotherapy.
Encapsulating functional natural enzymes within biocompatible matrices is a promising strategy for boosting therapeutic efficacy of enzymatic oncotherapy.[15,16] Ideally, biocompatible carriers for natural enzyme delivery should cause insignificant loss of enzymatic activities, allow substrate molecules to access the encased enzymes, avoid unwanted proteolytic degradation potentially inactivating natural enzymes, and so on. Among numerous enzyme carriers, an emerging class of hybrid organic–inorganic materials, termed metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), become the frontrunner because of the porous structures and excellent biocompatibility for catalytic activity preservation.[17–20] The microporous MOF encapsulation could also protect natural enzymes from surrounding inhospitable conditions, while provides a permeable and selective molecular channel for the diffusion of substrates and products.[8,21,22] Thus, MOFs could be advantageous over other matrices (i.e., silica) as biocompatible carriers of natural enzymes with excellent protective capability.[23] Recent studies have demonstrated the successful encapsulation of a variety of natural enzymes individually in MOF matrices with enhanced enzymatic activities and stability.[24–27] However, these attempts to integrate natural enzymes within MOFs have primarily been limited in structural design, stability, and (or) activity improvement, while their therapeutic utility for anticancer treatment has rarely been explored so far.
A well-established example of natural enzyme-based cancer therapeutic applications is the starvation therapy, in which glucose oxidase (GOx) catalyzes the oxidation of intratumoral glucose with molecular $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into gluconic acid and toxic $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ .[28] According to the Warburg effect, the survival and proliferation of cancer cells are more dependent on the glucose supply than normal cells.[29] The consumption of glucose via GOx-mediated oxidation could further decrease the intracellular adenosine triphosphate (ATP) production and thereby disrupt the cancer metabolic pathways, finally starving cancer cells to death and inhibiting the tumor growth. Unfortunately, the nature of the hypoxic tumor microenvironment (TME) exerts a big obstacle to the clinical translation of GOx-based starvation therapy.[28,30] As is also well known, catalase (CAT), a natural enzyme that is able to breakdown the overexpressed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in tumors into $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , could carry out the cascade reaction to replenish $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ for GOx-catalyzed glucose consumption.[18] In addition, the produced $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ from the GOx-catalyzed glucose oxidation could be in situ decomposed by horseradish peroxidase (HRP) to produce highly toxic reactive oxygen species (ROS) for cancer cell killing, which has already been adopted for glucose sensing.[11,31,32] Thus, we hypothesize that the combination of CAT, GOx, and HRP would constitute a highly efficient enzymatic cascade system, which has yet to be explored for anticancer treatment.
Herein, we design a plasmonic trienzyme-integrated MOF (plasEnMOF) nanoplatform with high-efficiency enzyme cascades for synergistic anticancer treatment (Scheme 1). The plasEnMOF nanoplatform comprises an Au nanorod $(\operatorname{AuNR})\ @$ zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) MOF core–shell structure embedded with three natural enzymes—CAT, GOx, and HRP. The three enzymes and the AuNR core were encapsulated in the ZIF-8 MOFs via a one-pot biomimetic mineralization process. The developed plasEnMOFs effectively integrate starvation, chemodynamic, and near-infrared-II (NIR-II, $1000{-}1700\ \mathrm{~nm}$ ) plasmonic hyperthermia trimodal therapy. Following intravenous administration into 4T1-tumor-bearing mice, the plasEnMOFs were preferentially accumulated at tumor sites via the enhanced permeability and retention (EPR) effect. The NIR-II plasmonic absorption of the small-sized AuNRs encapsulated in the ZIF-8 MOFs enables plasmonic hyperthermia therapy (PHT) at the therapeutic temperature of ${\approx}45\mathrm{-}55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ where normal tissue damage can be minimized. As such, the enzyme cascade reactions take place following the equations
$$
\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\stackrel{\mathrm{CAT}}{\rightarrow}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}+\mathrm{O}_{2}
$$
$$
\mathrm{Glucose}+\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{O}_{2}\xrightarrow[]{\mathrm{GOx}}\mathrm{Gluconicacid}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}
$$
$$
\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}+\mathrm{AH}_{2}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{HRP}}\cdot\mathrm{AH}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{O}_{2}}\mathrm{ROS}\left(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\right)+\mathrm{AH}^{+}+\mathrm{OH}^{-}
$$
where $\mathrm{AH}_{2}$ and ·AH are the reducing substrate and its free radical product, respectively.
The GOx encapsulated in the plasEnMOFs can be utilized for the starvation therapy via the GOx-catalyzed glucose consumption in which $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ can be continuously replenished by CATcatalyzed in situ decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ typically overexpressed in the hypoxic TME. The toxic ROS (·OH) produced from HRP-mediated enzymatic reaction affords for chemodynamic therapy (CDT). Additionally, we also confirm that the combination of the $\mathrm{\pH}$ decreases due to the GOx-catalyzed gluconic acid production and the local temperature rise as a result of NIR-II plasmonic heating makes the starvation therapy and CDT more efficiently. In vitro and in vivo experimental studies demonstrate that our plasEnMOFs achieve superior anticancer efficacy via the synergistic starvation/CDT/PHT trimodal therapy.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Synthesis and Characterization of plasEnMOFs
In this study, plasEnMOFs were synthesized using a one-pot biomimetic mineralization route, as depicted in Scheme  1A.[30,33] First, AuNRs with an edge length of 54.4 $(\pm11.9)\$ nm and a diameter of 9.4 $(\pm2.1)$ nm were obtained using a seed-mediated synthetic method well established in the literature (Figure S1, Supporting Information).[34,35] Then, an aqueous solution consisting of 2-methylimidazole (2-mIM), $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , and cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB)-capped AuNRs was added with a separate aqueous solution of GOx, CAT, and HRP at a mass ratio of 1:1:1.5 in a total protein concentration of $0.3~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ . The plasEnMOFs were formed due to the high affinity between the AuNR surface and 2-mIM with the simultaneous encapsulation of GOx, CAT, and HRP via the biomimetic mineralization process. The loading of enzymes can be effectively controlled by varying the initial concentration of enzymes. The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images, shown in Figure 1A,B, confirm the core/shell structure of the as-synthesized plasEnMOFs with a high encapsulation efficiency and uniform size distribution. The average diameter of the plasEnMOFs is determined to be 112.3 $_{(\pm28.0)}$ nm from the TEM data, much smaller than its hydrodynamic size (232.6 $(\pm2.5)$ nm) from the dynamic light scattering measurement (Figure S2, Supporting Information). This is due to the slightly hydrophobic surface of the ZIF-8 MOFs.[19] Additionally, the elemental mapping images of the plasEnMOFs confirm that the Au signal is located in the core surrounded by Zn and N signals of the ZIF-8 MOFs, further supporting the core/ shell structure of the plasEnMOFs (Figure 1C). The powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern of the as-synthesized plasEnMOFs contains two sets of characteristic peaks with the diffraction peaks at $38.1^{\circ}$ and $44.4^{\circ}$ assigned to the AuNR core (JCPDS #: 04–0784) and the other diffraction peaks assigned to the ZIF-8 MOF shell theoretically identified by simulated XRD data (Figure  1D).[36,37] The ZIF-8 component of both plasMOFs and plasEnMOFs displays high crystallinity with similar crystal structure, but the XRD pattern of the as-synthesized plasEnMOFs exhibits diminished intensity at the low angles $(5^{\circ}-11^{\circ})$ with respect to plasMOFs. This is due to the encapsulation of amorphous enzymes in the ZIF-8 MOFs structure, consistent with the previous report.[38,39] Thus, the XRD results demonstrate the successful synthesis of plasEnMOFs with good crystallinity.
AuNRs exhibit two plasmon bands, respectively, called transverse and longitudinal plasmon bands at 508 and $1066~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , and the longitudinal plasmon band was bathochromically shifted to $1115\ \mathrm{nm}$ for the as-synthesized plasEnMOFs owing to the increasing dielectric constant of the ZIF-8 MOF shell.[38,40] Also, the ZIF-8 MOF shell prevents AuNRs from aggregation in aqueous solutions or physiological environments, preserving the excellent NIR-II plasmonic properties. The encapsulation of enzymes was further examined using fluorescence micro­scopy. The three natural enzymes were labeled, respectively, with fluorescent probes fluorescein isothiocyanate (FITC), coumarin-6-sulfonyl chloride (6-CSC), and rhodamine B (RhB) isothiocyanate (RhBITC) to obtain FITC-GOx, 6-CSC-CAT, and RhB-HRP that emit green, blue, and reddish orange fluorescence (Figure S3, Supporting Information).[8,33] Confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM) images clearly display the confinement of the three natural enzymes in the as-synthesized plasEnMOFs, verifying the colocalization of three natural enzymes (Figure 1F). Additionally, we can see an intensity increase in the fluorescence emission of RhB tagged on the HRP at the excitation of $355~\mathrm{nm}$ relative to that at the excitation of $490\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure  1G). This is due to the fluorescence resonance energy transfer between the three fluorescent probes tagged on their respective enzymes, again supporting the successful encapsulation of the three enzymes. The loadings of GOx, CAT, and HRP in the plasEnMOFs were determined to be 0.44, 0.32, and $0.59~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mg}^{-1}$ , respectively, according to the reported fluorescence intensity method.[8] Thus, the mass ratio of GOx:CAT:HRP in the as-synthesized plasEnMOFs is slightly different from the initial mass ratio (1:1:1.5) added in the synthetic route, which may be due to the different encapsulation capacity toward the three enzymes. Powder XRD and TEM measurements show that the addition of enzymes does not significantly affect the crystal structure and morphology of the ZIF-8 MOFs (Figure S4, Supporting Information). We also demonstrate that an increase in the total enzyme concentration linearly increases the loading efficiency but reduces the encapsulation efficiency of enzymes with a steady-state value of $\approx40\%$ (Figure S5, Supporting Information). However, an enzyme concentration of $>0.3\ \mathrm{mg\mL^{-1}}$ fails to form a core/shell structure, as seen from the TEM image in Figure S6 (Supporting Information).[33] Thus, we utilized the total enzyme concentration of $0.3~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ for the following studies in this work.
To examine the porous properties of the as-synthesized plasEnMOFs, the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms were measured at $77\mathrm{K}.$ The results indicate that the plasEnMOFs display a type I isotherm with a steep increase in $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ uptake at a low relative pressure $(P/P_{0}<0.01)$ , similar to those of $\operatorname{AuNR}(\varnothing)$ MOFs (plasMOFs) and pure ZIF-8 MOFs both containing no enzyme (Figure 1H). The as-synthesized plasEnMOFs possess a high pore volume of $0.49\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of $937.61\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Compared with ZIF-8 MOFs and plasMOFs, the decreasing BET surface area in plasEnMOFs may be ascribed to the incorporation of AuNRs and enzymes in the cavities (Table S1, Supporting Information). The pore size distribution curve shows an average pore size of $1.077\ \mathrm{nm}$ for the plasEnMOFs similar to that in ZIF-8 MOFs or plasMOFs, suggesting the microporous nature.[41] Such a small porous size could block the accessibility of large macro-biomolecules (i.e., proteins, DNA, and proteolytic enzymes) from physiological environments to the functional enzymes in the MOF cavities and only allow their interactions with small molecules such as $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{O}_{2},$ and glutathione (GSH), whose sizes are much smaller than the pore size.[25] Thus, the superior microporous properties could effectively improve the stability and enzymatic activities of plasEnMOFs. Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra show similar spectral characteristics for ZIF-8 MOFs, plasMOFs, and plasEnMOFs, indicating no structural change of the ZIF-8 MOF component after enzyme loading (Figure S7, Supporting Information). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) shows an $\approx7.9\%$ loading of enzymes based on the weight loss in the temperature range of $215{\mathrm{-}}400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , again confirming the presence of enzymes in the plasEnMOFs (Figure S8, Supporting Information).[42] To improve the biocompatibility and mitigate the nonspecific adsorption of plasma proteins in in vivo biomedical applications, PEGylation was performed with 1,2-distearoyl-sn-glycero3-phosphoethanolamine-N-(methoxy(polyethyleneglycol)-2000) (DSPE-mPEG2000). The characteristic 951 and $1100~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ bands of $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-}$ and $1730\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ band of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ in the FTIR spectrum suggest the successful PEGylation of the plasEnMOFs (Figure S7, Supporting Information).[43] After PEGylation, the zeta potential of plasEnMOFs shifts to $-14.5\ (\pm0.4)\ \mathrm{mV}$ from the positive value $(+20.7$ $\mathrm{'\(\pm0.1)\mV)}$ of as-synthesized plasEnMOFs, and its hydrodynamic size increases to 272.6 (±7.0) nm (Figure 2A; Figure S2B, Supporting Information). Thus, the following experiments were conducted with PEGylated plasEnMOFs (also called plasEnMOFs hereafter).
## 2.2. In Vitro Evaluation of Enzymatic Activities of plasEnMOFs
We then individually evaluated the enzymatic activities of the three functional natural enzymes integrated in plasEnMOFs (Figure 2B). The enzymatic activity of $\operatorname{GOx}$ was examined based on GOx-catalyzed oxidation of glucose with $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into gluconic acid and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Equation  (2)). It is found that the $\mathsf{p H}$ value of the reaction solution consisting of plasEnMOFs and glucose decreases by 0.6 after $60\mathrm{min}$ reaction owing to the production of gluconic acid, while no obvious $\mathrm{\DeltapH}$ value decline occurs in the presence of plasEnMOFs and glucose individually (Figure S9, Supporting Information).[44] The direct diffusion of glucose via the pore channel and the acidic TME-induced degradation of ZIF-8 protective layer could take place to trigger the GOxcatalyzed oxidation of glucose.[22,39] The catalase activity of the plasEnMOFs was examined by monitoring $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production in real time from the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ decomposition reaction using a portable dissolved oxygen meter (Equation (1)). As shown in Figure 2C, the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration increases from 8.6 to $16.4~\mathrm{~mg~L^{-1}}$ after reaction for 500 s due to the breakdown of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ by catalase from plasEnMOFs, while keeps identical in the solutions of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and plasEnMOFs individually with the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration in the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ solution a little higher than that in the solution of plasEnMOFs only. In contrast, with the simultaneous presence of glucose and plasEnMOFs, the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration gradually declines and finally reaches a steady-state value even with the prolonging time exceeding $20~\mathrm{min}$ . The above results suggest that CAT of plasEnMOFs catalyzes $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ decomposition to produce $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , replenishing the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ supply for glucose consumption and improving the catalytic efficiency of GOx.[6] Further, the HRP catalytic activity from plasEnMOFs was assessed with $3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) as the molecular probe in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , which is oxidized to yield a characteristic absorption band at $652\ \mathrm{nm}$ due to the ROS generation.[45] As illustrated in Figure S10 (Supporting Information), a strong absorption band at $652~\mathrm{nm}$ characteristic of oxidized TMB can be obviously seen in the aqueous solution containing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $(10\times10^{-3}\textbf{M})$ and plasEnMOFs. Electron spin resonance spectra clearly display the quartet pattern with an intensity ratio of 1:2:2:1 characteristic of $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\mathrm{OH}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}+\mathrm{HRP}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}+$ plasEnMOFs in the presence of nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NADH) (Figure S11, Supporting Information).[28] These results provide direct evidence for the HRP-catalyzed ROS generation. Next, the effect of the $\mathrm{\pH}$ value on the enzyme cascades of the plasEnMOFs was investigated at three different $\mathrm{\pH}$ values (5.0, 6.2, and 7.4) (Figure  2D). The optical absorption spectra show the enhanced oxidation of TMB with the pH decrease from 7.4 to 6.2 and 5.0, consistent with the visual color change (inset of Figure 2D). This indicates that the acidic TME could benefit the ROS generation catalyzed by HRP in plasEnMOFs and thereby promote the corresponding CDT performance.[39,44] Further, we monitored the release of enzymes from plasEnMOFs at different $\mathrm{\pH}$ values, as shown in Figure 2E. The results show that the mild acidic conditions $\mathrm{(pH~5.0~}$ and 6.2) cause more release of enzymes compared with that at pH 7.4, accounting for the superior enzyme cascade activity of plasEnMOFs under acidic conditions. Thus, the following studies were conducted at the optimal $\mathrm{pH}~5.0$ value. Figure 2F shows the time-resolved optical absorption spectra of TMB in the presence of plasEnMOFs. It can be seen that the optical absorption at $652\mathrm{nm}$ of oxidized TMB is progressively intensified with prolonging reaction time up to $30~\mathrm{min}$ . Figure 2G,H confirms that an increase in the concentration of either plasEnMOFs or glucose causes more oxidation of TMB due to more ROS generation. The oxidization kinetics of TMB by plasEnMOFs was further followed in comparison with phosphate-buffered solution (PBS), plasMOFs, plasGMOFs, and plasGHMOFs (Figure  2I). The plasEnMOFs exhibit an enzyme cascade activity 4.5-fold higher than the plasGHMOFs; when GOx only exists or no enzyme is added, almost no obvious TMB oxidization is observed, suggesting no occurrence of enzyme cascades. The enzyme cascades proceed in the confined cavities of the plasEnMOFs, consequently yielding a high local concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in the vicinity of HRP with the auxiliary effects of CAT and GOx. The enzyme cascades driven by CAT, GOx, and HRP significantly boost the ROS generation and improve the overall enzyme catalytic efficiency with glucose consumption for starvation/CDT therapy of cancer.
## 2.3. Performance Stability and Photothermally Enhanced Enzyme Cascades
Next, we studied the protective properties of the ZIF-8 MOF shell in plasEnMOFs against external chemical agents and elevated environmental temperature. The plasEnMOFs were rigorously washed with PBS to remove the surface physically adsorbed enzymes. We found that $83.7\%$ of the enzyme cascade activity remains in the plasEnMOFs after storage for 8 days in $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ PBS at room temperature, while $51.0\%$ lost for the homogeneous mixture consisting of equivalent concentrations of GOx, CAT, and HRP (denoted as free enzymes) (Figure 3A). This suggests that the encapsulation of enzymes within the ZIF-8 MOF shell significantly enhances the long-term stability of enzyme activity.[25] The high structural stability of the plasEnMOFs was further evidenced by the insignificant changes of hydrodynamic size and zeta potential during the 7 day incubation in pH 7.4 PBS (Figure S12, Supporting Information). Our rationale is that the confinement of enzymes within the rigid ZIF-8 MOF shell prevents the protein denaturation from conformational change of enzymes.[22] The anti-interference ability is essentially important for the delivery and transportation of enzymatic drugs.[46] Proteolytic enzyme-induced degradation and chelating-compound-associated inactivation of enzymes are two major reasons for the poor stability of enzymes in vivo.[20] Thus, the stability of plasEnMOFs was investigated against two representative chemical agents—proteinase K and ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA) (Figure  3B-i).[22,45] The homogeneous mixture of GOx, CAT, and HRP in the PBS lost $\approx43\%$ of their overall enzymatic activity after being digested by proteinase K for $30\mathrm{min}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . In contrast, the enzymatic activity of plasEnMOFs keeps constant under the same condition, showing excellent tolerance against proteolytic enzymes. Similarly, no significant loss of the enzymatic activity takes place for plasEnMOFs in the presence of EDTA, whereas decreases by $\approx80\%$ of the enzymatic activity in the free enzymes. These results are consistent with the previous observations in the literature.[17,46–50] We infer that the hydrophilic modification of plasEnMOFs with DSPE-mPEG2000 also plays a crucial role in the preservation of enzymatic activity because it allows the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ substrate and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ product accessible to the encapsulated enzymes.[19] Thus, we demonstrate the excellent protective performance of the ZIF-8 MOF shell on the encapsulated enzymes in the present plasEnMOFs.
Previous studies showed that prolonged exposures of enzymes to high temperature $(>50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ could cause its conformational change and thereby lose the catalytic activity.[51,52] To gain insights into the influence of the photothermal effect on the enzyme cascades of plasEnMOFs, we assessed the enzyme cascade activities of plasEnMOFs at elevated temperatures compared with free enzymes. As shown in Figure  3B-ii, no loss of enzymatic activity was observed for plasEnMOFs as the temperature increased up to $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , while the enzyme cascade system consisting of free enzymes lost nearly $53\%$ of its original activity under the same condition. As the temperature increased up to $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the plasEnMOFs still maintained $\approx40\%$ of its original enzymatic activity, but $<10\%$ for the free enzymes. The enhanced thermal stability indicates that the microporous ZIF-8 MOFs resist thermal denaturation of encapsulated enzymes, consistent with previous reports.[22,38] The above results confirm that the present plasEnMOFs could tolerate high temperatures for extended duration with no significant loss in catalytic cascade activity.
Then, we modulated the enzymatic activity in the plasEnMOFs through the photothermal effect from the integrated NIR-II plasmonic AuNRs with a $1064\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser irradiation (Figure  3C). Figure  3D-i shows the concentration-dependent temperature rise of plasEnMOFs under $1064~\mathrm{{nm}}$ laser irradiation $(1.0\ \mathrm{W}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ). The temperature of the aqueous solution of $500~\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{\mL^{-1}}$ plasEnMOFs increased by $43.2^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , but only ${<}5~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for pure water during the $10~\mathrm{min}$ laser irradiation period. The plasEnMOFs also exhibited negligible deterioration of photothermal performance after four consecutive laser on/off cycles, indicating excellent photothermal stability (Figure  3D-ii). It can be seen that both photothermal temperature and catalytic efficiency of the enzyme cascade system increase with the increasing concentration of plasEnMOFs (Figure  3E-i). This indicates that the photothermal temperature could be regulated by varying the concentration of plasEnMOFs with the identical laser irradiation. When the temperature of the plasEnMOF cascade system was heated to $45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a $1064~\mathrm{{nm}}$ laser at which the three natural enzymes are not denatured, the catalytic efficiency of the enzyme cascades could be increased by ${\approx}95\%$ compared with that under normal condition. Nearly fourfold enhancement of the catalytic efficiency of the plasEnMOFs can be achieved under a $1064~\mathrm{{nm}}$ laser with respect to the free enzymes (Figure  3E-ii), much higher than that at the same temperature $({\approx}45^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . Our rationale is that the local plasmonic heating under a laser induces more rapid heat transfer beneficial to the enhancement of the catalytic efficiency of the plasEnMOFs compared with an external heating source. The local temperature rise enhanced release of enzymes was not observed during the laser irradiation at $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ (Figure S13, Supporting Information). Thus, we conclude that the enzyme cascade activity of plasEnMOFs can be manipulated for controllable oncotherapy.[6,10,11]
## 2.4. In Vitro Assessment of Anticancer Effects
We further evaluated the in vitro anticancer performance of plasEnMOFs as the enzyme cascade system. As shown in Figure 4A-i), plasMOFs without encapsulation of enzymes had negligible cytotoxicity against 4T1 cells. With the encapsulation of single (GOx), two (GOx plus HRP), or three (GOx plus CAT plus HRP) enzymes, the resulting plasGMOFs, plasGHMOFs, and plasEnMOFs all exhibited dose-dependent cell viability owing to the involvement of starvation and (or) CDT therapy. An impressive death of 4T1 cells is observed after incubation with plasEnMOFs at $60~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The cytotoxicity of plasEnMOFs is significantly higher than that of the systems containing one enzyme or two enzymes, indicating the presence of high catalytic efficiency in the trienzyme cascade system in cancer cells. Furthermore, it is revealed that both plasEnMOFs and plasMOFs exhibit significantly lower cytotoxicity toward normal NIH-3T3 cells than 4T1 cells (Figure 4A-ii).[11,44] To gain insights into the cell uptake behaviors of plasEnMOFs, we followed the 4T1 cells with CLSM imaging using plasEnMOFs made with RhB-HRP at different incubation times (0, 4, and $12\ \mathrm{h}$ (Figure  4B). The results demonstrate that plasEnMOFs are uptaken into 4T1 cells through an endocytosis pathway, and could escape from the lysosome upon prolonging incubation time. The plasEnMOF-associated ROS generation and ATP level change in 4T1 cells were then examined, respectively. First, intracellular ROS generation was probed with $^{2^{\prime},7^{\prime}}$ -dichlorofluorescein diacetate (DCFH-DA) as the molecular probe that is oxidized by ROS into green fluorescent 2,7-dichlorofluorescein (DCF) (Figure 4C).[6] Almost no green fluorescence is observed in 4T1 cells when HRP is not encapsulated, and the 4T1 cells incubated with plasEnMOFs exhibit higher green fluorescence intensity compared with plasGHMOFs, confirming the massive intracellular ROS generation by plasEnMOF-triggered trienzyme cascades. Further, the $1064~\mathrm{nm}$ laser irradiation greatly enhances the ROS generation owing to the plasmonic heating-induced photothermal effect (Figure  4C; Figure S14, Supporting Information). Figure 4D (top) shows that the intracellular ATP level is significantly reduced when treatments were performed with plasMOFs containing GOx, confirming glucose consumption by GOx-catalyzed oxidation.[28] Compared with the other treatments, plasEnMOF-treated 4T1 cells have the lowest intracellular ATP level with a further decrease under
$1064~\mathrm{{nm}}$ laser, which is attributed to the trienzyme-cascadeenhanced GOx catalytic efficiency.
Cytotoxicity and in vitro anticancer effects were subsequently examined through the cell counting kit-8 (CCK-8) cell viability assay, and calcein-AM/propidium iodide (PI) co-staining fluorescence imaging. After various treatments, the cell viability change of 4T1 cells is consistent with the ATP level change (Figure  4D, bottom). The treatment with plasEnMOFs plus laser causes the least cell viability of 4T1 cells. The calcein-AM/ PI co-staining experiments further evidence that cancer cell killing can be greatly enhanced through the starvation and CDT therapy combined with the PHT (Figure  4E). We also found that the treatment of 4T1 cells with plasEnMOFs caused significant damage to the mitochondrial membrane (Figure S15, Supporting Information).
## 2.5. In Vivo Biosafety, Pharmacokinetics, and In Vivo Anticancer Efficacy Assessment
The intriguing in vitro therapeutic effects of plasEnMOFs encouraged us to further examine the in vivo anticancer efficacy. Prior to in vivo experiments, we first performed the evaluation of in vivo toxicity. It is revealed that our plasEnMOFs did not cause swelling, rupture, and coagulation of red blood cells after blending for 3 h (Figure S16, Supporting Information). Results from the blood coagulation test also evidenced no obvious coagulation caused by the plasEnMOFs (Figures S17 and S18, Supporting Information). All these results indicate the good blood compatibility of the present plasEnMOFs. Subsequently, 4T1 tumor xenograft models of BALB/c female mice were established for in vivo experiments. After the tumor size reached $\approx100\ \mathrm{mm}^{3}$ , 4T1-tumor-bearing mice were randomly divided into eight groups $n=5$ per group, $\left(-\right)$ indicates no laser irradiation, $\left(+\right)$ indicates with laser irradiation): 1) PBS $\left(-\right)$ , 2) PBS $(+),3)$ plasMOFs $\left(-\right)$ , 4) plasMOFs $\left(+\right)$ , 5) plasGMOFs $\left(-\right)$ , 6) plasGHMOFs $(-),7)$ plasEnMOFs $\left(-\right)$ , and 8) plasEnMOFs $\left(+\right)$ . For the laser irradiation groups, the tumor-bearing mice were exposed to a $1064~\mathrm{{nm}}$ laser at $1.0\mathrm{~W~cm}^{-2}$ at $24\mathrm{~h~}$ post administration. To evaluate the photothermal effect of plasEnMOFs on 4T1-tumor-bearing mice, the tumor temperature was monitored in real time using an infrared thermal imager (Figure 5A). The tumor temperature increased rapidly with prolonging laser irradiation and reached to a steady-state temperature of $54.7^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ within $5\mathrm{min}$ for plasEnMOFs, which is enough for ablating cancer cell. In comparison, the tumor temperature of the PBS $\left(+\right)$ group only increased by $2.6~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
The pharmacokinetic profiles were then studied to determine the in vivo blood circulation and biodistribution of plasEnMOFs (Figure  5B). The biodistribution of plasEnMOFs was assessed at 4, 12, and $24\mathrm{~h~}$ post administration. The plasEnMOFs were primarily accumulated in the liver, spleen, and lungs resulting from the capture by the reticuloendothelial system.[44,53] The relative accumulation amount of the injected plasEnMOFs at the tumor site was $8.4\%$ ID $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at $24\mathrm{h}$ post administration, confirming the effective tumor accumulation via the EPR effect. In addition, the blood circulation of plasEnMOFs in the 4T1-tumorbearing mice was investigated. It is revealed that the blood circulation of plasEnMOFs follows a classic two-compartment model with moderate circulation half-times of $t_{1/2}\alpha=0.49\mathrm{~h~}$ and $t_{1/2}\beta=2.82\mathrm{~h~}$ (inset of Figure  5B).[54] Thus, we verify that our plasEnMOFs have excellent in vivo biocompatibility with effective accumulation in the tumor.
After the above treatments, the tumor volume and body weight of the mice bearing tumors were recorded every 2 days during a 14 day period. As displayed in Figure 5C,D, the tumor growth was remarkably restrained in plasEnMOFs $\left(-\right)$ and plasEnMOFs $\left(+\right)$ groups with minimal tumor growth in the combined treatment of plasEnMOFs plus laser irradiation. In contrast, the tumor rapidly grew in both PBS $\left(-\right)$ and PBS $\left(+\right)$ groups. A careful comparison further reveals the tumor growth suppression in the plasEnMOF-treated mice more effective than the other groups regardless of whether the laser irradiation is conducted. These observations are also supported by the digital photograph of tumors collected at day 14 from tumor-bearing mice receiving various treatments. The remarkable tumor growth suppression is attributed to the plasEnMOF-mediated high-efficiency enzyme cascades and plasmonic heating-associated photothermal effect: 1) the enzyme cascades driven by plasEnMOFs trigger effective starvation and CDT therapy due to high-efficiency glucose consumption and ROS generation, which can be further enhanced by plasmonic heating-induced photothermal effect; 2) the PHT therapy operated at ${\approx}45\mathrm{-}55~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a $1064~\mathrm{{nm}}$ laser irradiation selectively induces cancer cell death without obvious damage to the surrounding normal tissues; and 3) the enzyme cascades are boosted by the microporous properties of the ZIF-8 MOF shell with improved stability. No obvious differences of body weights of the mice receiving various treatments imply negligible systemic toxicity of plasEnMOFs. Further, hematological and blood biochemical analyses of the healthy mice treated with plasEnMOFs were conducted (Figures S19–S21, Supporting Information). The results show that the levels of all the hematological and blood biochemical parameters fall within the normal range, suggesting excellent blood compatibility and biosafety of plasEnMOFs.
To disclose the therapeutic efficacy and  anticancer mechanism, the tumor slices excised from 4T1-tumor-bearing mice at day 14 after various treatments were further analyzed by terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase dUTP nick end labeling (TUNEL) staining and hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) staining, respectively (Figure  5E,F). Severe apoptotic tumor cells were seen in the plasEnMOFs $\left(+\right)$ group, and the plasGMOFs $\left(-\right)$ and plasEnGHMOFs $\left(-\right)$ groups only led to moderate tumor damages, confirming much superior therapeutic effect from the enzyme cascades in plasEnMOFs. H&E images of major organs (heart, liver, spleen, lung, and kidney) showed no significant lesions or abnormalities from all these treatments (Figure S22, Supporting Information), verifying the excellent biosafety of the present plasEnMOFs.
## 3. Conclusion
A highly efficient natural enzyme cascade system has successfully been developed by encapsulation of NIR-II plasmonic AuNRs and three natural enzymes—GOx, CAT, and HRP within ZIF-8 MOFs. The developed plasEnMOFs integrate enzymatic cascade reactions and NIR-II plasmonic heating-induced photo­thermal effects for synergistic starvation/CDT/PHT trimodal cancer therapy. The catalytic activity of plasEnMOFs can be effectively modulated by varying the concentration and local temperature. Nearly fourfold enhancement of the catalytic efficiency of plasEnMOFs can be achieved with the plasmonic heating up to $45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a $1064~\mathrm{{nm}}$ laser, relative to the homogeneous mixture of free enzymes. The encapsulation of the three natural enzymes within ZIF-8 MOFs significantly promotes the glucose consumption and ROS generation owing to the enhanced trienzyme cascades, prominent microporous nature of the ZIF-8 MOFs, and excellent photothermal effects. We further demonstrate the enhanced catalytic activity and excellent protective capacity against extreme environments such as external chemical agents and high temperature of the present plasEnMOFs. Notably, the present plasEnMOFs are demonstrated with superior therapeutic efficacy on 4T1 tumor xenografts after intravenous administration combined with a $1064~\mathrm{{nm}}$ laser irradiation. The excellent therapeutic efficacy is attributed to the trimodal combination of starvation therapy, CDT and PHT. Thus, this study presents a paradigm for the construction of natural enzyme-based cascade systems with elaborately regulated catalytic activities for enzymatic cancer therapy.
## 4. Experimental Section
Mice for In Vivo Anticancer Studies: BALB/c female mice were obtained from Hunan SJA Laboratory Animal Co., Ltd. and used under appropriate protocols approved by the Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee of the Central South University (Protocol No. 2020sydw0724). To establish 4T1 xenograft tumor models, $B A L B/c$ female mice were subcutaneously injected with $2\times10^{6}$ 4T1 cells in $50~\upmu\up L$ of PBS into the hind leg of each mouse. When the tumor grew to ${\approx}100~\mathsf{m m}^{3}$ , the 4T1-tumor-bearing mice were established and used for in vivo experiments.
Statistical Analysis: Experiments were carried out in triplicate, and results were expressed as mean $\pm$ standard deviation (SD). Assignments to treatments and selections of fields of microscopic inspection were made at random. Statistical significance between the two groups was analyzed by two-tailed Student's t-test. The difference between three or more groups was analyzed by one-way analysis of variance. Difference with $\ast_{p}<0.05$ and $\because\mu<0.01$ , was considered statistically significant, and $\because0.001$ was very significant.
